  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Very warm start to the week, followed by mild conditions most of\n  the week. \n\n- Break down of the ridge coming next weekend.\n\n- Snow potential coming late next weekend and early next week.\n\nForecast high temperatures are in the top 10 on Monday for\nMissoula and Butte and in the top 15 for Kalispell, all locations\nwith more than 120 years of records. Then the ridge over the\nregion this week starts to flatten out leading to zonal flow, or\njust straight west to east flow. The only noticeable difference\nfor most people will be slightly stronger breezes in the mountains\nTuesday onward, and this pattern will continue through most of the\nweek. \n\nThe break down of the ridge pattern is on track for the end of the\nweak. This will be accompanied by a cold front and followed by\nsome precipitation. The details including the timing are pretty\nmessy in the ensembles right now. Most of the variability in the\nensembles is related to the depth of the trough that follows the\nridge break down with the timing of the break down a close second.\nRight now it looks like the cold front would cross the region late\nFriday into early Saturday, but that will likely change with\nfuture forecasts. So far the preponderance of the ensembles show\nthe break down being a dry cold front with little or any moisture,\nbut that changes over the weekend behind the front. \n\nLooking at the NBM, which is blend of many ensemble systems in the\nlong term, there's a 75% of at least some measurable snow in the\nmountains along the Continental Divide early next week. Median\nforecasts have a couple of inches in the Anaconda Pintlar,\nSapphire, and Bitterroot mountains with high elevations in Glacier\nNational Park and the Flathead National Forest along the\nContinental Divide picking up 4 to 6 inches. Along with the\nmoisture, afternoon temperatures early next week will be 20 or\nmore degrees cooler than their highs Thursday or Friday.",
